There's no bug here--Underflow is handled correctly on uint32_t. That is the reason uint32_t is chosen.

Try the following code to see:
```
uint32_t tick = 0xFFFFFFFFU;
uint32_t start = tick;
tick += 100;
uint32_t duration = tick - start;
printf("duration = %u\n", (unsigned) duration);
```

The result is `duration = 100`;

Using (0xFFFFFFFF - (start_tick - current_tick)) would introduce an off-by-one error in the calculation.

