If we define an input buffer of 16th bytes and use the 16th byte to simulate garbage appearance upon decryption, we can easily trigger the tag generation failure. The proposal in https://github.com/STMicroelectronics/stm32h7xx-hal-driver/issues/88#issue-3162577502 fixes that UB.

Hey all,

I've also came along this bug, I can reproduce this.

The HAL cannot handle payloads that are not multiple of wordsize if the latest word is not padded with zeros. In this case encrpytion/decrpytion is still correct, but the generated tag is wrong.

@GeorgeCGV I've tested your patch, but found two issues with it:
1. It does not work if the payload size is indeed a multiple of words, i.e. 12 bytes. In this case pad_bates=4 and you shift by 32 bits when processing the last valid word, effectively zeroing it.
2. I do not understand the check for CRYP_OPERATINGMODE_DECRYPT. Imo this padding has to be done for both encryption and decryption. You wont get a correct tag when encrypting without this.

Here is my proposed patch:

```patch
From 6f0ca77f98448dab01176a5fabd9502e64bad057 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lukas Ostendorf <lukas.ostendorf@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 9 Oct 2025 08:09:09 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Fix: CRYP HAL driver does not properly pad inputs that are no
 multiple of word-size

---
 Src/stm32h7xx_hal_cryp.c | 15 +++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Src/stm32h7xx_hal_cryp.c b/Src/stm32h7xx_hal_cryp.c
index 61d732e..d0720de 100644
--- a/Src/stm32h7xx_hal_cryp.c
+++ b/Src/stm32h7xx_hal_cryp.c
@@ -2913,6 +2913,7 @@ static HAL_StatusTypeDef CRYP_AESGCM_Process(CRYP_HandleTypeDef *hcryp, uint32_t
   uint8_t *pval;
   uint16_t outcount;  /* Temporary CrypOutCount Value */
   uint32_t DoKeyIVConfig = 1U; /* By default, carry out peripheral Key and IV configuration */
+  uint32_t mask[4] = {0x0FFFFFFFFU, 0x0FFFFFFU, 0x0FFFFU, 0x0FFU};

   if (hcryp->Init.KeyIVConfigSkip == CRYP_KEYIVCONFIG_ONCE)
   {
@@ -3087,8 +3088,18 @@ static HAL_StatusTypeDef CRYP_AESGCM_Process(CRYP_HandleTypeDef *hcryp, uint32_t
       /* Write the last input block in the IN FIFO */
       for (index = 0U; index < lastwordsize; index ++)
       {
-        hcryp->Instance->DIN  = *(uint32_t *)(hcryp->pCrypInBuffPtr + hcryp->CrypInCount);
-        hcryp->CrypInCount++;
+        if (index == lastwordsize -1) {
+          nolastpaddingbytes = npblb % 4U;
+          uint32_t tmp =  *(uint32_t *)(hcryp->pCrypInBuffPtr + hcryp->CrypInCount);
+          tmp &= mask[nolastpaddingbytes];
+          hcryp->Instance->DIN = tmp;
+          hcryp->CrypInCount++;
+        }
+        else
+        {
+          hcryp->Instance->DIN  = *(uint32_t *)(hcryp->pCrypInBuffPtr + hcryp->CrypInCount);
+          hcryp->CrypInCount++;
+        }
       }

       /* Pad the data with zeros to have a complete block */
-- 
2.47.2
